 * The two requests the whole idempotency mechanism exists for: one Idempotency Key,
 * arriving twice at the same moment rather than twice in a row.
 *
 * <p>Everything else about the mechanism has been asserted sequentially.
 * {@code WhatARepeatOfAKeyGetsBackTest} places each prior claim by hand and asks the port
 * what a repeat gets; {@link RetryingATransferRequestMovesMoneyOnceTest} sends the repeat
 * through the endpoint once the first request has finished. Both would stay green against a
 * design that read the table before writing it — the check-then-act every one of those
 * tests performs after the act is over. This is where that design fails, and it is
 * therefore the only test in the suite that the unique constraint is load-bearing for.
 *
 * <p>It is also the only place two branches of the production code can be reached at all.
 * A {@link hu.bankmonitor.payments.idempotency.RequestInProgressException} needs a claim
 * that is held while a second request asks about it, and the loser of a race to reclaim a
 * {@code FAILED} key needs a race; ticket 17 left both untested rather than reaching them
 * with a mock, which would have asserted that an {@code if} was typed correctly.
 *
 * <h2>The three ways this test could pass while proving nothing</h2>
 *
 * <p><b>A transactional test method.</b> {@link TransferScenario} is not annotated
 * {@code @Transactional}, so the claim each request commits is one the other request's
 * connection can see. Wrapped in one transaction, neither thread would observe the other's
 * claim, both would "succeed", and the race would be invisible rather than absent. The
 * price is emptying four tables by hand, which that class pays.
 *
 * <p><b>Threads that never overlap.</b> Two requests submitted together are not two
 * requests in flight together: against an in-memory database the first finishes in well
 * under the time it takes to dispatch the second, and the second then reads a settled
 * world and replays a stored {@code 201}. That failure mode produces <em>two</em>
 * {@code 201}s and one Transfer — which is a correct service answering correctly, and a
 * test that has proven nothing about concurrency.
 *
 * <p><b>An assertion that says "at least".</b> Each claim below is a count. A design with
 * no mutual exclusion at all reserves twice and writes two Transfers, and every
 * {@code hasSizeGreaterThan} or {@code anySatisfy} written against it passes.
 *
 * <h2>What forces the overlap</h2>
 *
 * <p>Two latches, and only one of them is the ticket's. {@code sendTogether} lines the two
 * requests up so they leave together, which is what makes them concurrent. It is not what
 * makes the outcome deterministic: the window in which the winner's key reads
 * {@code IN_PROGRESS} is one short transaction wide, and HTTP dispatch jitter is the same
 * order of magnitude. The same measurement is recorded against the reservation tests in
 * {@code docs/design-decisions/13-reserve-funds.md} — a start latch alone left a broken
 * lock order green.
 *
 * <p>{@link RowLockBarrier} is what holds the window open. The winner reaches a
 * {@code select … for update} on the source Account, which is inside the transaction that
 * closes its claim and after the claim itself has committed, and it is held there until
 * the other request has been answered. So the loser meets a key that is genuinely claimed
 * and genuinely unfinished, every run, and the {@code 409} below is the mechanism working
 * rather than the scheduler being kind.
 *
 * <p>Which of the two requests wins is the database's decision and this test does not have
 * one: the answers are collected as a pair and asserted as a pair.
 */
@Import(RowLockBarrier.class)
class ConcurrentRequestsUnderOneKeyExecuteOnceTest extends TransferScenario {

	private static final long SOURCE = 1L;

	private static final long DESTINATION = 2L;

	/** Twice over what one Transfer below costs, so that executing twice is possible. */
	private static final long BALANCE = 1_000_00L;

	private static final long AMOUNT = 100_50L;

	private static final String KEY = "0d1f6c1e-6b0a-4a5f-9f1a-2c3d4e5f6a7b";

	private static final String TRANSFER = """
			{"fromAccountId": 1, "toAccountId": 2, "amountMinorUnits": 10050}""";

	/** Generous, because it is only ever reached by a thread that is stuck, and then the test has failed. */
	private static final Duration BEFORE_GIVING_UP = Duration.ofSeconds(10);

	@Autowired
	private RowLockBarrier rowLocks;

	/**
	 * The guarantee, under the only conditions that can break it. One request creates the
	 * Transfer; the other is told the key is in progress and to come back, which is the
	 * advice that makes a client's retry policy safe rather than a second charge.
	 *
	 * <p>Three counts, and each rules out a different failure. Two {@code 201}s would mean
	 * the threads did not overlap, or that both were served from one claim. Two Transfer
	 * rows would mean the operation ran twice. A doubled Reserved Amount would mean it ran
	 * twice and left one of its Transfers somewhere this test does not look — the failure
	 * the row count alone cannot see.
	 *
	 * <p>The {@code 409} is checked for the URN and the {@code Retry-After} that tell it
	 * apart from the other {@code 409} this endpoint sends. They mean opposite things: this
	 * one is <em>wait</em>, and answering a lost race with the key-reused document would
	 * tell an honest client to give up on a Transfer that was about to succeed.
	 */
